  "term": "acid",
  "says": "The 303 line: a single saw (or square) into a resonant 24 dB low-pass whose cutoff is pushed by a fast-decaying envelope on every note - the squelch - with the resonance high enough to sing, an accent that pushes the envelope harder, and slides between notes; nothing else. Signature: one voice, an instant attack, harmonic, a fundamental in the bass, and a brightness that falls by many semitones over the first part of each note as the envelope closes. Measured 2026-09-14: the first version expected an instant attack and was refuted - the level peaks 0.2 s in, when the closing resonance sweeps past the strong low harmonics (measured 0.22 s at resonance 0.6 and 0.20 at 0.75); the onset is instant, the peak is the squelch, and the entry now expects the peak between 50 and 500 ms.",
